WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.890
Workflow, clear replies and calming angry customers.

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.290
This is the video for module 2 of Online Customer Support.

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:17.250
Good agents do not just reply to messages. They move each request through the same steps every time, so nothing is missed.

4
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:25.090
Most support work is writing. Clear writing saves time, because the customer understands the first time.

5
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.820
And a complaint handled well can turn an angry customer into a loyal one.

6
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:38.170
Step one, acknowledge quickly. Hello Kemi, thank you for your message. I am checking your order now.

7
00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:52.160
Step two, clarify in one message. Ifeoma asked: please send 1, your order number, 2, a photo of the shoes, 3, the size and colour you ordered. One message collected all the facts.

8
00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:59.890
Step three, check before you answer. Never guess. A wrong confident answer causes a second complaint.

9
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:12.210
Step four, solve it, or escalate. Refunds, threats and suspected fraud go to your supervisor, with a short summary: who, what, what you checked, what she wants, and the deadline.

10
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:18.240
Step five, confirm the fix, and later check it worked. Did the new pair fit well?

11
00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:23.080
Step six, log the case and change the label to Resolved.

12
00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:31.170
I will get back to you soon means nothing. Say a real time, and set a phone reminder for every time you promise.

13
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.630
Every reply has five parts. Greet her by name. Hello Amina.

14
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.930
Acknowledge. I understand you needed it for Saturday.

15
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:52.480
Give the answer, near the top, because customers on phones may not read to the end. Your parcel is in Abuja and will be delivered tomorrow before 4 pm.

16
00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:57.290
State the next step. The rider will call you before arriving.

17
00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:07.990
Close warmly. On chat, keep it to 2 to 4 short lines. And read every template once before sending, so you never send Hello, name in brackets.

18
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:15.320
Some words push customers away. Blame, a flat no, and vague times like as soon as possible.

19
00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:23.590
Replace blame with explanation. Replace can't with what you can do. And replace vague words with real times.

20
00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:31.840
The first draft reply said: yes we can do it, price depends, send details. That tells the customer nothing.

21
00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:38.170
The rewrite gives a starting price: from about 3,500 naira per head.

22
00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:45.730
For 80 guests, that is roughly 280,000 naira, before delivery and serving staff.

23
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:53.420
Then it lists the exact details needed and promises the quote within 2 hours. The customer can decide at once.

24
00:02:54.670 --> 00:03:09.500
Mariam's customer, Tola, wrote in capital letters: I paid 12,500 last week and nothing. Mariam listened and restated: I can see you paid 12,500 and have had to message three times.

25
00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:15.480
She apologised once: I am sorry, that is not the service you should get.

26
00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:25.270
She checked. The address was missing a street name. She offered choices: delivery today by 5 pm, or pickup from 11 am.

27
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:33.880
She thanked Tola, sent the rider update, and checked the next day. Tola went from calling them scammers to saying thank you.

28
00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:42.060
Do not blame the rider or the customer until you have checked. And never write calm down, or as I said before.

29
00:03:43.310 --> 00:04:01.140
Some cases are not yours to handle alone. Threats of harm, legal action or a public campaign, and product safety problems, such as a skin reaction or food illness, go straight to your supervisor or the owner. So do refunds and suspected fraud. Log every one.

30
00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:13.040
To remember. Use the six steps every time, and ask all your questions in one numbered message. Write in five parts, with real times. Calm complaints with LAST.

31
00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:20.000
And if a customer complains in public, reply once, briefly and politely, then move to a private chat.

32
00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:35.480
Your turn. Run one real or realistic case through all six steps. Write at least 6 templates and rewrite 3 weak replies. Then write a LAST reply to an angry message, and ask a friend how it feels to read.

33
00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:37.770
Continue with the lessons below.
